module

public import StacksFrontier.Homology.DerivedComplete.NoetherianCompleteIdealPowers

@[expose] public noncomputable section

/-!
# Eventual inverses for the augmentation of a constant tower

Let `V` be an object of a category and `G : ℕᵒᵖ ⥤ C` an inverse system equipped with a map
`φ : (Functor.const ℕᵒᵖ).obj V ⟶ G` out of the constant system at `V`. This file settles when
such a `φ` is a pro-isomorphism in the sense of
`NoetherianAdicCompletion.HasEventualInverses`.

The transition maps of a constant system are identities, so the first half of
`NoetherianAdicCompletion.HasEventualInverses` reads `φ.app (op m) ≫ s = 𝟙 V`: it asks for a
**retraction** of `φ.app (op m)`, not merely a map back. That is
`NoetherianAdicCompletion.hasEventualInverses_const_iff`, and it is the reason the question
"does the augmentation of a constant tower have eventual inverses?" is the question "is
`φ.app (op m)` a split mono for arbitrarily large `m`?".

## The criterion

A retraction at level `m` is not by itself enough: the second half asks `s ≫ φ.app (op n)` to be
the transition map `G.map`, and a retraction chosen blindly need not satisfy it. The correction is
cheap. Given

* a retraction `s₀` of `φ.app (op m)`, and
* **any** factorisation `t ≫ φ.app (op n) = G.map (homOfLE hm).op` of the transition map through
  `V`,

the single map

`s := s₀ + (𝟙 - s₀ ≫ φ.app (op m)) ≫ t`

satisfies both halves at once. `𝟙 - s₀ ≫ φ.app (op m)` is the idempotent complementary to the
summand `s₀` splits off, so it is killed by `φ.app (op m)` and the first half survives untouched;
the second half comes out because naturality of `φ` on a constant source says exactly
`φ.app (op m) ≫ G.map (homOfLE hm).op = φ.app (op n)`, which is what makes the two error terms
cancel. This is `NoetherianAdicCompletion.hasEventualInverses_const_of_forall_exists`.

In a pretriangulated category both inputs are vanishing statements about the triangles
`W m ⟶ V ⟶ G.obj ⟨m⟩ ⟶ (W m)⟦1⟧` presenting the augmentation:

* `ψ m = 0` gives the retraction, by `Triangle.yoneda_exact₂` at the identity of `V`;
* `G.map (homOfLE hm).op ≫ δ n = 0` gives the factorisation, by `Triangle.coyoneda_exact₃`.

That is `NoetherianAdicCompletion.hasEventualInverses_const_of_triangles`, which reduces the
pro-isomorphism to two maps being zero and nothing else.

## What this is for

The tower in play in [Stacks, Tag 0A0K] part (1) is the adic reduction tower
`m ↦ V ⊗^𝐋 𝒪/ℐᵐ⁺¹` and `V` is a dual Koszul complex `(Kⁿ)^∨` on a chart, at a *fixed* Koszul
index `n`. There the two triangles are the derived tensor of `V` with
`ℐᵐ⁺¹ ⟶ 𝒪 ⟶ 𝒪/ℐᵐ⁺¹`, so `W m = V ⊗^𝐋 ℐᵐ⁺¹` and the two hypotheses read

* `ψ m : V ⊗^𝐋 ℐᵐ⁺¹ ⟶ V` is zero, and
* `V ⊗^𝐋 𝒪/ℐᵐ⁺¹ ⟶ (V ⊗^𝐋 ℐⁿ⁺¹)⟦1⟧`, the transition map followed by `δ n`, is zero.

Both hold for large `m`, and the reason is that `(Kⁿ)^∨` is an iterated cone of the `f_i^{n+1}`
rather than the unit: multiplication by each `f_i^{n+1}` is null-homotopic on a Koszul complex
(`Koszul.koszulSeq_smul_nullHomotopic_and_span_le_annihilator`, and in sheaf form
`Stacks.Sheaves.KoszulGlobalSections.koszulSmulNullHomotopic`), so the ideal `J` generated by
the `f_i^{n+1}` annihilates the identity of `V` in the derived category, hence annihilates every
cohomology module of `RHom(V, X)` for every `X`. A bounded complex with `I`-power-torsion
cohomology is derived `I`-torsion, and for such an `E`

`colim_m Hom(𝒪/ℐᵐ⁺¹, E) ≅ H⁰(E)` and `colim_m Hom(ℐᵐ⁺¹, E) = 0`,

the second following from the first and the long exact sequences of
`ℐᵐ⁺¹ ⟶ 𝒪 ⟶ 𝒪/ℐᵐ⁺¹`. Taking `E = RHom(V, V)` kills the first map, and `E = RHom(V, V ⊗^𝐋 ℐⁿ⁺¹)`
kills the second.

That derived-torsion colimit identification is available over a commutative ring — it is
[Stacks, Tag 0954], `Stacks.LocalCohomology.localCohomology_isHomotopyColimit_derivedHomPowers`
of `StacksFrontier/RingTheory/LocalCohomology/TorsionColimit.lean`, together with
`Stacks.LocalCohomology.localCohomologyHomologyIsoColimitExt` — but **not** for sheaves of
modules on a ringed site, where there is no derived `ℐ`-torsion functor at all; what the
ringed-site tree has is the per-object Čech coreflection
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.bijective_comp_cechAugmentation_of_derivedPowerTorsion` of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/FormalGeometry/DerivedCompletionRinged/CechCoreflection.lean`,
which is the *input* to such a functor and not the functor.

Nothing below is conditional and nothing below carries a `@[stacks]` attribute: these are the
general category-theoretic reductions, and the vanishing inputs are hypotheses.

## Main results

* `NoetherianAdicCompletion.hasEventualInverses_const_iff` — for a constant source, eventual
  inverses means a retraction at arbitrarily large levels, compatibly with the transition maps.
* `NoetherianAdicCompletion.exists_eventualInverse_const` — the correction itself: a retraction
  and any factorisation of the transition map are combined into a single eventual inverse.
* `NoetherianAdicCompletion.hasEventualInverses_const_of_forall_exists` — a retraction plus any
  factorisation of the transition map suffices; the two are corrected into one map.
* `NoetherianAdicCompletion.hasEventualInverses_const_of_triangles` — in triangulated form, two
  vanishing maps suffice.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0921](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0921)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A0K](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A0K)
-/

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated Opposite

universe u v

namespace NoetherianAdicCompletion

section Unfolding

variable {C : Type u} [Category.{v} C] {V : C} {G : ℕᵒᵖ ⥤ C}

/-- **Eventual inverses out of a constant system ask for a retraction.** The transition maps of
`(Functor.const ℕᵒᵖ).obj V` are identities, so the first half of
`NoetherianAdicCompletion.HasEventualInverses` says `φ.app (op m)` is a split mono with the
chosen `s` as retraction. -/
public theorem hasEventualInverses_const_iff (φ : (Functor.const ℕᵒᵖ).obj V ⟶ G) :
    HasEventualInverses φ ↔ ∀ n : ℕ, ∃ (m : ℕ) (h : n ≤ m) (s : G.obj (op m) ⟶ V),
      φ.app (op m) ≫ s = 𝟙 V ∧ s ≫ φ.app (op n) = G.map (homOfLE h).op := by
  simp only [HasEventualInverses, Functor.const_obj_obj, Functor.const_obj_map]

end Unfolding

section Correction

variable {C : Type u} [Category.{v} C] [Preadditive C] {V : C} {G : ℕᵒᵖ ⥤ C}

/-- **A retraction at level `m` and any factorisation of the transition map through `V` combine
into an eventual inverse.** The combination is
`s₀ + (𝟙 - s₀ ≫ φ.app (op m)) ≫ t`; see the module documentation. -/
public theorem exists_eventualInverse_const (φ : (Functor.const ℕᵒᵖ).obj V ⟶ G) {n m : ℕ}
    (hm : n ≤ m) {s₀ t : G.obj (op m) ⟶ V} (hs₀ : φ.app (op m) ≫ s₀ = 𝟙 V)
    (ht : t ≫ φ.app (op n) = G.map (homOfLE hm).op) :
    ∃ s : G.obj (op m) ⟶ V,
      φ.app (op m) ≫ s = 𝟙 V ∧ s ≫ φ.app (op n) = G.map (homOfLE hm).op := by
  have hnat : φ.app (op m) ≫ G.map (homOfLE hm).op = φ.app (op n) := by
    have := φ.naturality (homOfLE hm).op
    simpa using this.symm
  refine ⟨s₀ + (𝟙 _ - s₀ ≫ φ.app (op m)) ≫ t, ?_, ?_⟩
  · have hkill : φ.app (op m) ≫ (𝟙 _ - s₀ ≫ φ.app (op m)) = 0 := by
      rw [Preadditive.comp_sub, Category.comp_id, ← Category.assoc, hs₀]
      simp
    rw [Preadditive.comp_add, hs₀, ← Category.assoc, hkill, zero_comp, add_zero]
  · rw [Preadditive.add_comp, Category.assoc, ht, Preadditive.sub_comp, Category.assoc, hnat]
    simp only [Category.id_comp]
    abel

/-- **The criterion for eventual inverses out of a constant system.** For every level `n` it is
enough to find a later level `m` at which `φ` has a retraction, together with *any*
factorisation of the transition map `G.map (homOfLE hm).op` through `V`. -/
public theorem hasEventualInverses_const_of_forall_exists (φ : (Functor.const ℕᵒᵖ).obj V ⟶ G)
    (h : ∀ n : ℕ, ∃ (m : ℕ) (hm : n ≤ m) (s₀ t : G.obj (op m) ⟶ V),
      φ.app (op m) ≫ s₀ = 𝟙 V ∧ t ≫ φ.app (op n) = G.map (homOfLE hm).op) :
    HasEventualInverses φ := by
  rw [hasEventualInverses_const_iff]
  intro n
  obtain ⟨m, hm, s₀, t, hs₀, ht⟩ := h n
  obtain ⟨s, hs₁, hs₂⟩ := exists_eventualInverse_const φ hm hs₀ ht
  exact ⟨m, hm, s, hs₁, hs₂⟩

end Correction

section Triangulated

variable {D : Type u} [Category.{v} D] [HasZeroObject D] [HasShift D ℤ] [Preadditive D]
  [∀ n : ℤ, (shiftFunctor D n).Additive] [Pretriangulated D] {V : D} {G : ℕᵒᵖ ⥤ D}

/-- **The eventual-inverse criterion in triangulated form.** Present the augmentation at each
level by a distinguished triangle `W m ⟶ V ⟶ G.obj ⟨m⟩ ⟶ (W m)⟦1⟧`. Then `φ` has eventual
inverses as soon as, for every `n`, some later level `m` has both

* `ψ m = 0`, the map `W m ⟶ V` presenting the defect at level `m`, and
* `G.map (homOfLE hm).op ≫ δ n = 0`, the transition map composed with the structural map of the
  triangle at level `n`.

No bound of any kind is used, and neither hypothesis mentions a limit. -/
public theorem hasEventualInverses_const_of_triangles (φ : (Functor.const ℕᵒᵖ).obj V ⟶ G)
    (W : ℕ → D) (ψ : ∀ m : ℕ, W m ⟶ V) (δ : ∀ m : ℕ, G.obj (op m) ⟶ (W m)⟦(1 : ℤ)⟧)
    (hT : ∀ m : ℕ, Triangle.mk (ψ m) (φ.app (op m)) (δ m) ∈ distTriang D)
    (hvanish : ∀ n : ℕ, ∃ (m : ℕ) (hm : n ≤ m),
      ψ m = 0 ∧ G.map (homOfLE hm).op ≫ δ n = 0) :
    HasEventualInverses φ := by
  refine hasEventualInverses_const_of_forall_exists φ fun n => ?_
  obtain ⟨m, hm, hψ, hδ⟩ := hvanish n
  obtain ⟨s₀, hs₀⟩ := Triangle.yoneda_exact₂ _ (hT m) (𝟙 V) (by
    simpa [Triangle.mk] using hψ)
  obtain ⟨t, ht⟩ := Triangle.coyoneda_exact₃ _ (hT n) (G.map (homOfLE hm).op) (by
    simpa [Triangle.mk] using hδ)
  exact ⟨m, hm, s₀, t, hs₀.symm, ht.symm⟩

end Triangulated

end NoetherianAdicCompletion

end
